knowledge "design" {
    purpose "The design lenses this project applies to its own changes: DRY and SOLID, each built on the engineering rulings that carry it and assessed per change, not mandated."
}

ruling "dry" {
    pack "design"
    statement "The DRY lens. Its subject is duplicated KNOWLEDGE, never text that looks alike; ruling::engineering::abstraction-needs-two-real-cases and ruling::engineering::reuse-before-reinvention carry it, so read those. Applying it means answering, for this change, whether two sites now encode one decision that must move together. If not, the honest answer is that the lens does not bite; inventing a shared helper to satisfy it is itself a defect. Process duplication counts too: a role's model policy or packs copied into a brief is one decision stored twice."
}

ruling "solid" {
    pack "design"
    statement "The whole SOLID lens. Single responsibility (ruling::engineering::one-reason-to-change) is the letter this project leans on most. Open/closed: a new case arrives by adding to a seam, not by editing the decision that dispatches over it. Liskov: an implementation must work wherever its interface is expected; a provider that answers differently from its siblings breaks the seam even when it compiles. Interface segregation: no consumer satisfies obligations it never uses. Dependency inversion: the decision depends on the seam, not on what is behind it. Name the letters the change touches and the assessment; touching none is a complete answer. Adding an abstraction to score better is a defect, not compliance."
}

ruling "a-lens-assessment-is-evidence-not-proof" {
    pack "design"
    statement "A recorded lens assessment says the question was asked and what the answer was. It proves nothing about whether the design is DRY or SOLID, is not a review verdict and clears no other obligation: a failing contract stays failing. Treat another agent's assessment as its claim and form your own."
}

ruling "lens-applicability-follows-responsibility" {
    pack "design"
    statement "Who applies these lenses follows the role's responsibility, never its model size. The orchestrator applies them while designing, decomposing and integrating, where duplicated decisions and misplaced responsibilities are born. A reviewer assesses the letters the change touches. A bounded worker owes no assessment: it gets the requirement and the design constraint already decided, follows it and reports a conflict, without re-deriving the architecture. A design-heavy worker assignment names its lenses in its statement. Fewer lenses never exempt anyone from the assignment's requirements, scope or approval rules."
}
